  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I didn't open the dashboard or wait for the rule to run, so I haven't checked that either works.\n\n**Fields:** the logs data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. The fields that matter for traffic are `response` (the status code, stored as a string), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url` and `request`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-9fa32c2a Web logs overview\" (id `eff-9fa32c2a-web-logs-overview-4f9f270a4e1e`), with a default range of the last 7 days. It has four panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Responses by status code (pie)\n- Average bytes by file extension\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-9fa32c2a 5xx spike\" (id `47ed393a-90a8-4b6e-b765-c1ff385a6354`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It checks every minute.\n- It fires when more than 10 documents with a `response` starting with 5 arrive in the last 5 minutes.\n- The tool creates rules disabled, so I enabled it.\n- I didn't attach any action, such as a notification, so a firing only shows up as an alert in Kibana.",
